  "id": "scapular-depression",
  "name": "Scapular Depression",
  "definition": "A strict open-chain scapular isolation whose concentric phase depresses one scapula against overhead elastic resistance while the humerus remains beside the torso and the elbow remains extended. The initial contract admits only McCabe et al.'s directly prescribed unilateral Theraband fixture. Lower trapezius is the sole mover because it is the only measured muscle in that experiment that can produce depression; the larger measured serratus activity is treated as scapular stabilization, not relabeled as depression. Pectoralis minor was not measured and is not inferred into the role roster. The source measured EMG rather than three-dimensional scapular kinematics, so dips, press-ups, weight-relief raises, scapular pull-ups, straight-arm pulldowns, passive hangs, and other resistance geometries remain outside this narrow contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "mccabe-2007-below-90-scapular-exercises"
  ],
